<div class="article-content prose" style="margin-top: 40px; padding-top: 40px; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);">
<h2>The Conversion Formula</h2>
<p>To convert your airline miles or points balance into an estimated travel value, we use a simple multiplication formula based on the Cents Per Point/Mile (CPP) valuation you select:</p>
<div class="formula-box">
  <span class="formula-name">Estimated Travel Value =</span>
  <span class="formula-expression">Total Miles × (Valuation in Cents ÷ 100)</span>
</div>

<h3>Calculation Examples</h3>
<div class="example-box">
  <h4>Worked Example 1: Airline Miles to Dollars</h4>
  <p>You have an accumulated miles balance of 60,000 airline miles and want to estimate their travel purchasing power before booking an award flight.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Miles balance:</strong> 60,000 miles</li>
    <li><strong>Assumed value per mile:</strong> 1.5¢ per mile (editable demo assumption)</li>
    <li><strong>Calculation:</strong> <code>60,000 miles × (1.5¢ ÷ 100) = $900</code></li>
    <li><strong>Estimated travel value:</strong> <strong>$900</strong></li>
  </ul>
  <p><em>Limitation: This represents modeled travel purchasing power, not an official cash buyout, cash withdrawal, or live airline ticket quote.</em></p>
</div>

<div class="example-box" style="margin-top: 16px;">
  <h4>Worked Example 2: Transferring Flexible Bank Points to a Partner (Chase UR → Hyatt)</h4>
  <p>When modeling bank credit card rewards transferred to partner airlines or hotels, evaluate the transfer in two distinct steps. <strong>Do not conflate the valuation of a bank point with the valuation of a partner point</strong>, and do not multiply bank points directly by partner unit values without accounting for the transfer ratio.</p>
  <p><em>Setup: You hold 50,000 Chase Ultimate Rewards points. You evaluate transferring them to World of Hyatt, where you assume hotel points yield an illustrative 1.5¢ per Hyatt point.</em></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Step 1 — Point Transfer:</strong><br>
      <code>50,000 Chase points × 1.0 (illustrative demo ratio) = 50,000 World of Hyatt points</code><br>
      <em>Note: The 1:1 transfer ratio is an illustrative demo input for eligible premium cards. Transfer ratios, promotional bonuses, and card-tier rules vary across accounts. Always verify the live ratio in your Chase portal before initiating an irreversible transfer.</em>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Step 2 — Valuation at Partner Rate:</strong><br>
      <code>50,000 Hyatt points × (1.5¢ per Hyatt point ÷ 100) = $750</code>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Estimated Travel Value:</strong> <strong>$750</strong> (or $0.015 per original Chase point under a 1:1 ratio).</li>
  </ul>
  <p><em>Impact of alternative ratios: If an account or partner promotion utilized a different transfer ratio (for example, 4:3, yielding 37,500 Hyatt points), the estimated travel value would be <code>37,500 × $0.015 = $562.50</code> ($0.01125 per Chase point). Always calculate both steps separately.</em></p>
</div>

